{
  "updated": "2026-11-09",
  "slug": "questions-to-ask-a-financial-advisor",
  "title": "Questions To Ask a Financial Advisor Before You Hire",
  "metaDescription": "Ask these questions before hiring a financial advisor: how they are paid, their duty to you, fees on $10,000 and any disciplinary history.",
  "h1": "Questions To Ask a Financial Advisor",
  "cardBlurb": "Eight questions from the SEC's Form CRS to ask an advisor or broker before you hire, plus how to check the answers against public records.",
  "introText": "The questions to ask a financial advisor before hiring are the conversation starters on [Form CRS](https://www.investor.gov/CRS), the Client Relationship Summary prescribed by the [Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/working-investment-professional). At ModernWallet, we start from that official list because each firm's summary uses similar headings in a similar order, so you can compare firms side by side. Registered broker-dealers and registered investment advisers are required to give retail investors this summary.\n\nAsk how you will pay, whether an advisory or brokerage service fits you, and what legal duty the person owes you. Ask how much of $10,000 would go to fees, how they choose investments, what their qualifications mean, how the firm makes money and whether anyone has a disciplinary history. Before any of that, [Investor.gov](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/working-investment-professional) says the most important question is whether the person is registered with the SEC or with a state securities regulator.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "The Questions To Ask a Financial Advisor",
      "body": "There are eight questions to ask a financial advisor, and each one appears on Form CRS or in Investor.gov's guidance about it. The wording below comes from the Form CRS of one firm registered as both a broker-dealer and an investment adviser, retrieved on November 9, 2026. Under each question is what a clear answer should cover.\n\n1. \"Given my financial situation, should I choose an investment advisory service? Should I choose a brokerage service? Should I choose both types of services? Why or why not?\" The [Investor.gov Form CRS page](https://www.investor.gov/CRS) says brokers and advisers can both advise on buying a stock or an index fund and on whether to sell or hold it later. A clear answer explains which type of service, or which combination, fits your goals, preferences and needs.\n2. \"How will you choose investments to recommend to me?\" The Investor.gov [investment advisers page](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/working-investment-professional/investment-advisers) says an adviser typically gives ongoing advice and monitors how your investments perform and how they line up with your overall objectives. A clear answer says what the person looks at when picking an investment and how they review it afterward.\n3. \"What is your relevant experience, including your licenses, education and other qualifications? What do these qualifications mean?\" Investor.gov warns that initials after a name do not make one person more qualified than another, and that some initials mean the person can only sell certain products. A clear answer names each credential and says what it lets the person sell or do for you.\n4. \"Help me understand how these fees and costs might affect my investments. If I give you $10,000 to invest, how much will go to fees and costs, and how much will be invested for me?\" The Form CRS notes that fees and costs reduce what you make on your investments over time, and that you pay them whether you make or lose money. A clear answer gives two dollar figures that add up to $10,000.\n5. \"What are your legal obligations to me when providing recommendations as my broker-dealer or when acting as my investment adviser?\" Investor.gov says investment advisers are required to act in your best interest and not put their interest ahead of yours. A clear answer states the duty for each role the person could play with your account, and our guide on [what a fiduciary is](/guides/what-is-a-fiduciary/) explains the adviser side of that duty.\n6. \"How else does your firm make money, and what conflicts of interest do you have?\" Follow it with the form's own prompt: \"How might your conflicts of interest affect me, and how will you address them?\" Investor.gov says the way advisers make money creates some conflicts with your interests, and those conflicts can affect the advice you get. A clear answer names each conflict and what the firm does about it.\n7. \"How do your financial professionals make money?\" The firm whose form is quoted here says its professionals can be paid through commissions, advisory fees or both, and that higher commissions, costs or fees could lead to higher pay for the professional. That is one firm's disclosure, so ask the person in front of you to describe their own pay.\n8. Do you or your firm have any legal or disciplinary history, and for what type of conduct? Investor.gov says a Form CRS has to state whether the firm and its financial professionals have reportable legal or disciplinary history. A clear answer matches what the form says and explains the conduct behind any entry.\n\nAsk for the answers in writing where you can. Written answers are easier to line up against the firm's Form CRS after the meeting."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Public Record Checks on Registration and History",
      "body": "The answers about registration and disciplinary history can be checked against public records before you hire anyone. Investor.gov calls registration with the SEC or a state securities regulator the most important question to settle first. Its [Working with an Investment Professional page](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/working-investment-professional) offers a free and simple search that shows whether an investment professional is licensed and registered.\n\nYou can also run a name through [adviserinfo.sec.gov](https://adviserinfo.sec.gov) and through BrokerCheck from the [Financial Industry Regulatory Authority (FINRA)](https://brokercheck.finra.org). If the person lists CFP (Certified Financial Planner) after their name, look the name up on the [CFP Board's verification page](https://www.cfp.net/verify-a-cfp-professional). Investor.gov also points to a FINRA list showing that some credentials are easy to obtain and others are hard-earned.\n\nNext, set the person's spoken answers beside the firm's [Form CRS](https://www.investor.gov/CRS). Investor.gov says every relationship summary covers the firm's services, fees and costs, conflicts of interest, required standard of conduct, and legal or disciplinary history, using similar headings and order of topics.\n\nIf a spoken fee or conflict answer does not match the form, ask which one is correct and get the reply in writing. Our guide on [how to choose a financial advisor](/guides/how-to-choose-a-financial-advisor/) covers the wider comparison between firms once each candidate has answered the same eight questions."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Red Flags and What To Do",
      "body": "Three answers are reasons to stop and look at other candidates, based on what Investor.gov says and what Form CRS must contain.\n\n- The person is not registered with the SEC or a state securities regulator. Investor.gov says it is \"really risky to invest with someone who isn't licensed,\" so move on to another candidate.\n- The firm will not give you a Form CRS. Registered broker-dealers and registered investment advisers are required to give one to retail investors, so ask once in writing and keep looking if it still does not arrive.\n- The record shows legal or disciplinary history the person cannot explain. Investor.gov lists that history, and the type of conduct behind it, among the things to weigh before selecting an adviser, so an unexplained entry is a reason to choose someone else.\n\nA clean record does not settle the other questions on the list. Keep the fee, duty and conflict answers in your comparison even for a candidate with no history."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Questions To Ask a Financial Advisor in Special Situations",
      "body": "If you want more than investment recommendations, add questions about planning services and about which accounts and products the person can offer. Investor.gov says advisers may also give advice about market trends or asset allocation, or offer financial planning services. It also tells you to weigh the services and products you need against what the adviser can provide and any limits on it.\n\n- Do you offer financial planning services, or advice on asset allocation?\n- Which services and products can you provide, and what can you not provide?\n- Can your firm offer both advisory and brokerage accounts? Investor.gov notes that many firms are registered as both a broker and an adviser and offer both types of account.\n- How much will I pay for services, and how much for each transaction?\n\nFor retirement savings, use the same eight questions and add which retirement account types the person handles. This page does not cover retirement account rules or contribution limits."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Who These Questions Are Not For and What Would Change the List",
      "body": "These questions fit someone choosing an ongoing relationship with an adviser or broker. Investor.gov says advisers typically provide ongoing advice, and that the adviser's fee is typically based on the value of all the assets in your account, with other fees and costs possible on top. If you need only a one-time answer to a single money question, read our guide on [whether a financial advisor is worth it](/guides/financial-advisor-worth-it/) before you sit through a full interview.\n\nTwo things would change this list. If the SEC changes the conversation starters printed on Form CRS, we would rebuild the list from the new form. If a candidate fails the registration question, you can skip the rest of the list for that person, because Investor.gov treats registration as the first test."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Our Takeaway",
      "body": "Ask every candidate the same eight questions from the SEC's relationship summary, then compare their written answers with their Form CRS and their public record. Look elsewhere if the person is not registered or cannot explain a disciplinary record.\n\nThe same list works whether the person is an investment adviser, a broker or part of a firm that is both. Copy these questions to ask a financial advisor, send them before the first meeting, and check the person's registration with the free search on [Investor.gov](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/working-investment-professional)."
    }
  ],
